  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ for $g(x)={\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{\u2032}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To compute ${g}^{\u2032}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({u}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(({e}^{x})^{3})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
